00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.230
Now, I'll walk you through
the basic messaging

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.910
architecture at Hike.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:45.690
Also we'll be discussing
a few key components

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.320
of our infrastructure
and the Google

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.550
is helping us to
manage it better.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:52.780
Sure, next slide.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:55.490
So Hike messaging runs
on [INAUDIBLE] protocol

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:57.310
over a [INAUDIBLE]
TCP connection

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.440
between client and a server.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.530
On a given day at peak, tens
of millions of connections,

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.740
clients connect to our
[INAUDIBLE] servers.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.300
So for that, you require
a good load balancer

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.570
in the front layer, which was
served by [? edge ?] proxy

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:12.190
in our case.

00:08:18.146 --> 00:08:19.930
To connect to
[? edge ?] proxy layer,

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:24.220
we have a DNS [INAUDIBLE],,
which further divides into five

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:24.970
different CNAMES--

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.390
[INAUDIBLE], respectively.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:30.420
Each of the CNAMEs
further resolves

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.309
to cluster of [? edge ?]
proxy IP addresses.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:36.070
Along with this, we used to
maintain a list of public

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:40.929
static IP addresses which were
hardcoded in clients if DNS

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:44.140
resolution fails, which used
to happen because of poor

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.910
connectivity of all the
[? ISBs. ?] [? Edge ?] proxy

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.930
in turn forwards the connection
to [INAUDIBLE] server and also

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.040
to SSR termination.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:55.480
At the start of
every connection,

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.340
client sends a connect
package with UID

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:59.830
and password for authentication.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:01.897
Connection gets
established only after

00:09:01.897 --> 00:09:02.980
successful authentication.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.280
So for that, you
require an Auth service.

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.230
After that, whatever
messages client sends,

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.960
we used to include them
into the front queues

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:12.580
for asynchronous processing.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.480
There are job
processes which used

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:18.790
to consume these messages
from these queues

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.700
and process the message
sent by the client.

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.860
To send the message
back to the recipient,

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.190
these job processors used
to submit the message

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.097
to message delivery service,
which in turn sends [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:29.097 --> 00:09:31.680
the message based on whether the
recipient is connected to any

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.910
of the [INAUDIBLE] or not.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:36.700
So this is how basic
messaging works.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.090
So where exactly Google
helps us in this?

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.590
For that, I would
say, at every layer,

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:43.690
whether a front end, a
business [INAUDIBLE],,

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.158
or a backend database.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:52.880
While we were learning
GCP, we did a small POC

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.780
and found out some major
advantages which prompted

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:59.060
us to move to GCP stack.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:02.300
Some of the
advantages are, first,

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:07.300
Google provides infinitely
scalable frontend layer

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:13.340
GLB, which can scale quickly
on network or connections both.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.930
It suits perfectly to our
needs, and also we found latency

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:21.030
improvement of around 1.7x as
compared to [? edge ?] proxy

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.480
layer.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:24.950
Second, superior hardware.

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:29.390
We ran a simple test of Redis
benchmark on a similar hardware

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:33.920
machines and found throughput
to be 1.7x more on the GCP

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.730
hardware as compared to our
previous cloud provider.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.735
Combining this fact with
managed instanced group, which

00:10:40.735 --> 00:10:44.210
can autoscale on CPU load
or any other metrics,

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.640
it provides a
simpler architecture

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.060
with cost benefits.

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:53.110
Third, Google provides a lot
of APIs, [INAUDIBLE],, ML,

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.572
and data processing which
are a lot easier to integrate

00:10:55.572 --> 00:10:56.530
if you're on GCP stack.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.180
So this is how our
architecture looks

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.790
like after migrating to GCP.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.422
As you can see, we don't
have to maintain a list

00:11:07.422 --> 00:11:08.630
of [? edge ?] proxy machines.

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:11.790
We don't have to maintain
a different CNAMEs and also

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:14.480
a list of public static IPs.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.690
GLB just provides you
one public static IP,

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:21.150
which is behind a DNS and
also hardcoded into client

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:22.400
if DNS resolution fails.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:27.980
As you can see, not all
the processing layer

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.170
has been replaced by a
managed instance group which

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.170
can scale on CPU or load.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:36.110
This helps us in
saving a lot of cost.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.420
On a database layer, we
have a lot of footprint

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:41.600
on [? edge ?] base, which we
are now migrating to Bigtable.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.880
It not only provides
a better throughput

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:47.130
but also takes away the
manageability from us.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.970
So this is the
[INAUDIBLE] architecture,

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:52.530
how our messaging looks like.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.600
Now I would like to invite my
colleague Gaurav to deep dive

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:58.350
into two specific
companies-- job processors

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:00.160
and address book database.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.070
GAURAV ARORA: Thank you, Aditya.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:09.080
Hello, everyone.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:09.580
I'm Gaurav.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:12.280
I'm director of engineering
in Hike Messenger in India.

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.882
Today I'd like to talk
about a couple of things.

00:12:18.882 --> 00:12:20.340
The first one is
the job processors

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.130
and then it's the address
book, as Aditya mentioned.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.700
So let's start with
job processors.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.160
So a job processor's
considered an important part

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:30.720
of any messaging
application as this

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:34.020
is where most of the
business logic resides.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.150
Now when we started
building our application,

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.610
we started with a
monolithic architecture

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:42.060
where all the components were
in a single deployable unit,

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:45.300
and all the processing
was done synchronously.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:47.917
Now this approach is usually
the quickest to develop.

00:12:47.917 --> 00:12:49.500
And that's one of
the main reasons why

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.570
we choose to do it this way.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.250
But very soon, we
hit scale and we

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:56.320
started facing the
pitfalls of this approach.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:59.670
Now, we had frequent downtimes.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.650
We weren't able to scale
independent components

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.060
separately.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:04.830
Continuous deployment
became a hassle.

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:08.040
And the code base was so
large and complex that none

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.856
of the developers were
able to understand it well.

00:13:10.856 --> 00:13:12.480
So then we broke the
entire system down

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.640
into multiple components.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.330
And the process of delivering
an incoming message

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.850
was made asynchronous.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.630
So this is how it looks like.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.560
This is how it looked
like back then.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:27.210
So all the incoming
messages were

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.190
passed through a
sharding function, which

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:31.960
helped to identify a particular
queue on the basis of message

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:36.220
type and the user
ID in the message.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:39.090
Now, each of these queues
had a single consumer

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:42.720
associated with them which picks
up messages from these queues

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.570
and tries to deliver
them to the end users.

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.880
Now, as you would know, one
of the main requirements

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.490
from any messaging application
is an ordered delivery

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:51.979
of messages.

00:13:51.979 --> 00:13:53.520
There can't be more
than one consumer

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.830
for each of the queues
or else this guarantee

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:58.270
won't be maintained.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:01.642
Now this architecture helped
us in separation of components,

00:14:01.642 --> 00:14:03.600
each of which could now
be independently scaled

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:05.685
up or down as
[INAUDIBLE] required.

00:14:05.685 --> 00:14:08.310
It was much better scalable than
the previous monolithic system

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:09.490
that we had.

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:11.490
However, the system was
still a bit difficult

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.750
to scale up as we were
limited by the maximum number

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.490
of consumers, which
always had to be

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:20.680
equal to the number of
queues in the system.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.554
In other words, in order
to scale the system,

00:14:23.554 --> 00:14:25.470
we needed to increase
the number of consumers.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.011
And to increase the number of
consumers, the number of queues

00:14:28.011 --> 00:14:29.790
had to be increased
which in turn required

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.250
a change in producers so that
the newly added queues are

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.170
taken into account by
the hashing function

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:36.500
that you see up there.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.980
Now, another major issue that
we faced with this approach

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.050
was the [? data ?] starvation.

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:45.560
So messages that get
queued in the end

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:49.560
are always delivered
in the end as well.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:52.310
So older messages
get delivered first.

00:14:52.310 --> 00:14:55.070
Now since these queues are
shared by multiple users,

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:57.845
it means what happened that
a user's messages end up

00:14:57.845 --> 00:14:59.720
in a queue which is
already full of messages.

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:01.670
And the user
experience of that user

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:03.129
won't be great
because that person

00:15:03.129 --> 00:15:04.670
would have to wait
for much longer as

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.440
compared to other users.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.282
So we re-architectured
the whole solution

00:15:08.282 --> 00:15:09.990
and moved to something
that we internally

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:12.860
call as per user job queue.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.200
This is how it looks like.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.580
So again, all the incoming
messages go through a function.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:22.190
And a queue is decided
only on the basis

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:24.570
of message type in this case.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.170
And so the producer's
job is only to put it

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:30.170
into an appropriate queue on
the basis of message type.

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.300
From there, these
messages are picked up

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.100
and placed into one
of the user queues,

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:38.010
depending on which user
it has to be delivered to.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:41.600
Now, the limitation still
exists that in order

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:44.349
to deliver the messages
in the order in which they

00:15:44.349 --> 00:15:46.640
were received, we can still
have only a single consumer

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:47.870
per queue.

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:50.510
But since each user has
its own independent queue,

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:53.226
scaling this architecture
up only requires

00:15:53.226 --> 00:15:55.100
us to increase the number
of consumers, which

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:58.260
can be easily put on autoscale.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.291
Now this architecture
in particular

00:16:00.291 --> 00:16:02.540
helped us scale to more than
a billion messages a day.

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:06.050
And we hope that it will
continue to scale up

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:08.610
when we get more scale.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:11.810
So let's move on
to the other one,

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:14.378
other object, which
is the address book.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.030
So address book is one of the
biggest data store in Hike

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:26.300
and has undergone a lot of
transformations over the years.

00:16:26.300 --> 00:16:28.220
The use case for address
book is very simple.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:31.870
Whenever a user joins Hike, the
entire contact book of the user

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:34.409
is synced back to our servers.

00:16:34.409 --> 00:16:36.950
And this information is later
used to serve out different use

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:38.250
cases.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:42.650
The read pattern of this data
is actually two dimensional.

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:44.555
First one is a
forward-looking query,

00:16:44.555 --> 00:16:48.440
wherein you want to
query all the users'

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:50.810
data that are in a particular
person's contact book.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:52.600
And the other is a
reverse relation,

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:54.560
wherein you want
all the users who

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.980
have this particular user
in their address book.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.130
Now when we started
building this messaging app,

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:01.822
NoSQLs were very popular.

00:17:01.822 --> 00:17:02.780
That was the buzz word.

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:05.119
And MongoDB was one of
the most popular ones

00:17:05.119 --> 00:17:06.660
back in those days.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:10.670
So we also decided to build
our address book using MongoDB.

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:12.680
Now back in those
days, MongoDB was

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:15.920
on its MMAPv1 engine, which used
to acquire our database level

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:17.630
logs.

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:20.359
So initially, the way we
architected the system was we

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:24.220
had one Mongo database for
forward-looking relations

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:27.109
and one database for
reverse relations.

00:17:27.109 --> 00:17:30.590
However, since the use
case was very write heavy,

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.080
we frequently had scale
issues with MongoDB

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:35.690
because of its DB level logs.

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:39.080
So as an immediate remedy,
what we decided to do was we

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:43.160
sharded the Mongo cluster
into multiple instances.

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:45.170
That helped for a while,
but the rate at which

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:46.760
we were growing
back then, we knew

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:50.090
that we had to do something
completely different.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:54.640
Now if you look at the data
structure of our address book,

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.370
the schema is pretty fixed.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:18:00.010
You have user A whose
address book we've picked up.

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:01.580
There is user B in
the address book.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:04.620
And there is a name and
maybe some other metadata.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.980
So since the schema
was fixed, we

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:11.740
decided to move the entire
load to the traditional RDBMS.

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:13.640
We chose My SQL for that.

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:17.701
And My-- yeah, I think--

00:18:17.701 --> 00:18:18.200
yeah.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:19.890
We moved to MySQL.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:23.270
Now MySQL served very well
for us for multiple years,

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:25.310
and when we required
more capacity,

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:26.990
we used to scale up the machine.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:29.330
We were able to vertically
scale our machines up

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:33.470
to about three billion
rows in a single table.

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:36.980
And basically the table looked
like there was a user A,

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:39.800
there is user B, and then
there is a column for metadata.

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:44.300
Now since we required read
patterns on both user A and B,

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:47.420
we simply made indexes
on both of them.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:51.890
At one point, since we are
vertically scaling this up,

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:54.700
we ended up with a machine
with 104 gigabytes of RAM.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:57.260
And that was the max
that our service provider

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:59.180
could provide at that point.

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:02.020
So going forward, since
we were still growing,

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:02.890
we had two options.

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:05.000
We could either shard
our MySQL workload

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.280
into multiple
instances, or we could

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:10.470
move to a database which
provided sharding by default.

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:13.670
So we chose the latter,
and we moved on to HBase.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:16.970
So when we started
migrating to HBase,

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:20.530
the initial design that we
chose was a wide schema,

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:23.900
wherein the user ID whose
address book we've picked up,

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:26.630
the user ID became the row
key, and all the contacts

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:29.510
became different
columns in the same row.

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:34.490
Now since a user's address book
can have as little as maybe 10

00:19:34.490 --> 00:19:37.110
or 15 contacts and it can go
as big as tens of thousands

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:40.770
of contacts with very frequent
deletions and very frequent

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:44.020
updates, HBase cluster was
going through very frequent

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:47.380
[INAUDIBLE] compactions,
which wasn't desirable at all.

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:49.960
So we knew we had to
change something again.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:52.100
The next-- what we did
with the HBase cluster

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:53.970
was, instead of
all a wide schema,

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.280
we moved it to a
tall schema, wherein

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:00.522
the combination of user ID A and
user ID B became the row key,

00:20:00.522 --> 00:20:01.980
and the metadata
was the only thing

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:03.900
that was left for columns.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:06.140
This architecture served
really well for us.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:07.350
It's horizontally scalable.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.510
We haven't really felt
any need to replace that.

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:18.300
But when we moved onto
GCP back in late 2017,

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:21.030
we evaluated
Bigtable and realized

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:24.840
that the throughput in Bigtable
was about 1.5 to 1.7 times

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.510
faster than the throughput
that we were getting in HBase.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:31.770
So we decided to migrate
everything on to Bigtable.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:33.840
One reason to move
that was latencies.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:36.510
Another reason was it takes away
all the manageability effort

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:37.815
from us.

00:20:37.815 --> 00:20:40.620
Basically, Google does
everything under the hood.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:45.420
So earlier on HBase, whenever
a machine used to go down,

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:48.390
a data node used to go down due
to hardware failure or whatever

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:50.800
other reason, we used to
see spikes in latencies.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.220
But ever since
migrating onto Bigtable,

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.320
we haven't really
seen any such spikes.

00:20:56.320 --> 00:20:58.300
So Bigtable is
serving really well

00:20:58.300 --> 00:20:59.800
for us and we hope
that it continues

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:01.900
to serve the same way.

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:04.960
So that's all I had for
the address book journey.

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:11.780
What's next?

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:13.520
We're actually
doing multiple POCs

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:16.490
for migrating use cases onto
some of the managed services

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:17.990
that Google provides.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:20.150
Some of the ones
we are evaluating

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:21.680
include Spanner and Bigtable.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.830
So we hope to move
very soon onto those.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:26.680
Then we're also
evaluating Kubernetes

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:28.700
and trying to
integrate Spinnaker

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:32.330
for continuous delivery
of our code basis.

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.830
Now with that, I'd
like to call on stage

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:36.410
[? Sugit ?] from
Google, who will

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:39.440
talk about the basic building
blocks of the analytics

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:40.546
pipeline.

00:21:40.546 --> 00:21:43.090
[? SUGIT: ?] Hello.

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:45.860
Thanks a lot, Gaurav.

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:48.540
So let's talk about
data and analytics.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:51.920
So when you're managing
hundreds of millions of users,

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:53.979
having a solid and
stable data analytics

00:21:53.979 --> 00:21:56.270
pipeline becomes really
important because you're always

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:59.750
running AB test, you're always
collecting a lot of events,

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:02.930
and you want to process them
and take data driven decisions

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:03.950
as quickly as possible.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:11.580
So this is how our typical data
analytics pipeline, a streaming

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.220
one, could look.

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:15.947
On the left-hand
side of the screen,

00:22:15.947 --> 00:22:18.030
you can see that you have
all your sources, right?

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:20.370
So you have your
analytics events.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:22.690
You have all your logs.

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:25.720
You have some external
leaders sources coming in.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:30.200
And then you have your
transactional databases.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:33.532
All of them can be put
in a messaging queue

00:22:33.532 --> 00:22:35.240
if you are building
a streaming pipeline,

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:41.430
or you can just do a
batch processing as well.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.390
And then after that, you need
to have an ETL processing

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.930
mechanism to transform that data
and finally dump all of that

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:50.690
into a data warehouse.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:53.450
And once your data
is in data warehouse,

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:55.250
you can start
building dashboards

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:57.687
for all your
internal teams, start

00:22:57.687 --> 00:23:00.020
your business analysis, started
working on your business

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:03.830
intelligence, start getting
more insights out of that data.

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:06.562
And then your own machine
learning folks start--

00:23:06.562 --> 00:23:09.020
as soon as they see the data,
they'll start building models

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:10.820
and start working on them.

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:20.220
Let's see how this data
analytics pipeline maps to GCP.

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:24.120
And this is one of the ways
you can build a data analytics

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:27.940
pipeline, a streaming
one onto GCP.

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:30.360
So you have your analytics
events which you can directly

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:35.050
push into a messaging queue.

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:38.640
You can just store
your logs into the GCS,

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.530
which is the cloud storage.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:44.020
External sources,
you can sync, again,

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:49.110
GCS with your-- put in your
external data, third party data

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:50.339
onto to Cloud Storage.

00:23:50.339 --> 00:23:52.380
And then you have databases
with a lot of choices

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:57.420
around Spanner or MySQL Postgres
or managed SQL databases.

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:05.010
For messaging queue, we have
our scalable Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:07.530
We use it internally
for a lot of use cases.

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:13.710
And it can scale to very,
very high injection speeds.

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:16.240
Then we have our
managed Hadoop offering,

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:18.570
which is the cloud
data proc in case

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.090
you want to build
a batch pipeline

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:26.340
and start directly
processing your logs there.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:29.190
Once your data is--

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:33.510
inside pops up, you can
build a cloud dataflow

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:39.370
pipeline which is just an
Apache Beam runner onto GCP.

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:40.920
So it's, again, open source.

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.500
It's a managed runner, and
it scales automatically.

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:45.990
You can just define
the set of nodes,

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:50.130
and it will scale up and scale
down based on how quickly

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:54.270
and how much money you have
to process that, the money you

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:56.430
are willing to spend on that.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:57.480
And then very recently--

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.150
I think yesterday-- we
announced Cloud Composer which

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:04.250
is our managed Apache Airflow.

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:07.730
You can use Airflow to
control and schedule

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:11.607
your DAGs, which are
your data pipelines, when

00:25:11.607 --> 00:25:13.190
do you want to run
them and how do you

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:15.080
want to organize them together.

00:25:18.802 --> 00:25:20.260
And then there are
other ways also.

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:24.150
Like you can directly
import your Spanner.

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:26.774
We've recently announced
Spanner backups,

00:25:26.774 --> 00:25:28.690
and you can import them
into BigQuery as well.

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:32.260
So that, again, directly
goes to your data warehouse.

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:34.350
Now Apache Beam,
using Apache Beam,

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:38.130
you can dump all of
that data into BigQuery.

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:43.110
You can either stream or
batch upload it into BigQuery.

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:50.210
Then for managing dashboards,
we have our data studio,

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:52.340
which can directly
connect to BigQuery.

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:56.900
And then it can help you build
your internal dashboard which

00:25:56.900 --> 00:26:00.070
you can expose to your teams.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:03.050
For business intelligence, we
have managed Jupyter Notebook's

00:26:03.050 --> 00:26:04.160
environment.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:05.990
We have a Cloud Datalab.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:09.620
We also have Kaggle,
wherein you can--

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:11.270
if you want to try
out third party data

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:14.420
or build some interesting
insight onto that,

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:15.900
you can use that.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:19.640
And then finally, we have the
cloud machine learning engine,

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:22.340
which lets you train
your machine learning

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:26.970
models at scale, so
less fully managed.

00:26:26.970 --> 00:26:31.140
So you can easily read
data from BigQuery,

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:35.420
and it will automatically
scale out, scale up, scale down

00:26:35.420 --> 00:26:37.160
based on your needs.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:41.360
So all of this end to end
is completely managed.

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:43.490
There are a lot of components.

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:46.170
But usually I think
with Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:48.740
and BigQuery, it is quite
straightforward and easy,

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:51.900
and it's good connectivity.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:57.090
So now let's see how was
Hike's journey, right?

00:26:57.090 --> 00:27:00.530
So when you're migrating and
building your data pipeline,

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:01.700
it is--

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:04.040
and you're coming in from
a different environment,

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:05.990
be it on-prem or a
different cloud provider,

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:07.490
it is not always
so straightforward.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:09.800
There are their own challenges.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:12.120
And you have your
own experiences.

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:14.330
So I would like
to call in Manjeet

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:16.880
to talk about their
journey around how

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:22.460
did they manage to process
around 2 petabytes of data

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:24.130
as quickly as possible onto GCP.

00:27:28.369 --> 00:27:30.490
MANJEET SINGH: Thank
you, [? Sugit. ?]

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:32.530
My name is Manjeet Singh.

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:34.510
And I'm leading the
Data [INAUDIBLE] team

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:36.230
at Hike Messenger.

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:39.060
And today I'll be discussing
about the journey of data

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:41.950
pipeline at Hike
Messenger and also

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:46.180
how Google technologies like
Dataproc, Dataflow, GCS,

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:48.050
BigQuery benefited us.

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:51.640
Hike is a very data-driven
company where every decision

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.650
is taken based on data.

00:27:53.650 --> 00:27:57.590
So if you talk of data on
an average on a daily basis,

00:27:57.590 --> 00:28:01.060
we are generating
approximately 14 to 15 billions

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:04.840
of row, which is coming
around 4 to 5 TB of data.

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:07.930
And in all, we have a
accumulated more than 2

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:10.560
petabytes of data.

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:13.580
Now let's move to
the slide where

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:17.410
we can have the look at
the older data pipeline.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:19.840
So this is our
how it looks like.

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:22.450
As Aditya explained, we have
a different kind of servers

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:24.520
like [? SMTP ?] servers,
[INAUDIBLE] servers,

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:25.630
analytic servers.

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:28.720
And we have the clients which
are talking to the servers,

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:31.180
and clients generating
their own analytics

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:33.190
logs which are going
to anaylytic servers.

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:36.430
And we were saving
into the log files.

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:39.100
Then other servers, like
[? SMTP ?] and [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:41.890
servers, they were generating
their own analytic logs

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:43.960
and it was going into log files.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:47.760
Then we had the crontab setup
on each and every machine,

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:50.410
which were copying our
[INAUDIBLE] data into the cloud

00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:51.130
storage.

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:53.530
Then further, we
had crontab jobs

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:55.570
which are copying data
from the cloud storage

00:28:55.570 --> 00:28:58.160
to our in-house Hadoop cluster.

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:00.640
So for batching,
we were using Hive.

00:29:04.194 --> 00:29:09.850
And it was running on our
own in-house Hadoop cluster.

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:11.350
And all the
aggregated tables were

00:29:11.350 --> 00:29:13.480
going into the
[INAUDIBLE] database

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:15.790
for the faster data access.

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:19.780
And [INAUDIBLE] tool
which we were using.

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:22.780
And [INAUDIBLE] was a
[INAUDIBLE] license tool

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:24.370
which we were using.

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:27.680
But with this pipeline, we
were facing many challenges.

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:32.000
So let's have a look at the
challenges that we were facing.

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.040
So we need to set
up analytics crong

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:38.970
on every machine, whenever
we are on a new machine.

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:42.710
And there was a loss of logs
in case of machine failures.

00:29:42.710 --> 00:29:47.820
And replaying of misfiles
was very messy stuff.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:50.030
And in the older
data pipeline, we

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:52.340
were processing data
at [INAUDIBLE] level.

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:56.330
So it was not as real
time as we were expecting.

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:59.450
And as we had the
in-house Hadoop cluster,

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:01.670
so we were managing it manually.

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:03.740
It was not an easy job.

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:06.050
And it was all of
us running clusters

00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:09.960
so there was a huge
cost associated with it.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:16.800
And once we migrated to GCP, so
we improved upon our existing

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:21.510
older data pipeline by adopting
various Google technologies

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:22.710
and other technologies.

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:25.780
So let's have a look at
the existing data pipeline

00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:27.580
we have at Hike Messenger.

00:30:27.580 --> 00:30:29.800
So this is how it looks like.

00:30:29.800 --> 00:30:32.820
So now instead of generating
data into log files,

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:35.100
we are generating
data into Kafka.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:38.580
And then further, we have the
in-house Kafka consumers which

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:40.800
are consuming data from Kafka.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:44.340
And then we are cleaning
and denormalizing and then

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:46.080
generating the ORC files.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:49.590
And at every 15 minutes, we
are copying these ORC files

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:51.420
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:53.340
And then we have the Hive.

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:55.110
And we're using
Hive for batching.

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:59.300
And Hive is running on
Dataproc and in Dataproc,

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:01.890
as Dataproc is managed
by Google itself.

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:08.010
So in Dataproc, we are using
two kind of machine instances.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:09.225
Like one is the fix worker.

00:31:09.225 --> 00:31:11.100
Another one is the
preemptive [? old ones. ?]

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:12.141
These are the autoscales.

00:31:12.141 --> 00:31:15.570
We have set up the
min and max limit

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:21.120
which get scale out and scale
in based on our settings.

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:22.950
And all the summarized
tables we are

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:24.660
pushing into Google BigQuery.

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:26.770
It is going through
Google Pub/Sub.

00:31:26.770 --> 00:31:29.160
And then we have the machine
learning and DS algorithm

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:32.467
which are running on our high
cluster and Google BigQuery.

00:31:32.467 --> 00:31:34.800
And then we have the Tableau
as the [? BA ?] tool, which

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:37.500
is talking to Google BigQuery.

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:41.380
And then we got many advantages
after moving to this pipeline.

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:44.340
So let's have a look.

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:47.280
Now, after moving to
this existing pipeline,

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:49.890
we need not to set
up any analytics

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:53.220
of crons for copying data
from the servers to the cloud

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:54.270
storage.

00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:56.400
And then fetching data
from Google BigQuery

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:57.600
becomes super fast.

00:31:57.600 --> 00:31:59.650
It is more than 10x faster.

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:02.520
And now we are processing
data at 15-minute levels,

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:09.120
so we are moving towards
very near-real-time analysis.

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:11.790
And now we don't have
any in-house cluster,

00:32:11.790 --> 00:32:14.840
which is we are using for
data processing now as we

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:16.950
are using Google Dataproc.

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:18.540
So it is managed by Google.

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:21.312
And we are using it in
an autoscaling manner

00:32:21.312 --> 00:32:22.520
using the preemptable images.

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:27.480
And then we have the Kafka.

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:29.250
And we can use the
Kafka for building

00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:32.470
many data-driven services.

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:37.060
And we are still improving upon
our existing data pipeline.

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:45.900
And so this is how our future
pipeline we will be having.

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:47.850
And in future, what
we're trying to do

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:51.260
is instead of having
in-house Kafka consumers,

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:53.330
we are using Dataflow.

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:56.670
And Dataflow will be
consuming data from the Kafka.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:59.670
And then what we
are trying to do

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:03.030
is this will be a
metric-driven pipeline.

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:06.090
We are using a rule
engine for generating

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:09.150
the metrics in the data flow
itself, in the transform phase

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:10.200
of the data flow.

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:12.750
And then we are aggregating
and generating the second level

00:33:12.750 --> 00:33:15.120
of metrics, and
again aggregating,

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:18.360
and then finally we are pushing
into Google Bigtable and Google

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:20.225
BigQuery.

00:33:20.225 --> 00:33:22.200
In Bigtable, we are
putting some data

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.660
which is being used by our
application for real time

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:25.620
access.

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:29.470
And then BigQuery will be
our final data warehouse

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:32.320
where our machine learning
stuff and Jupyter Notebooks

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:34.540
and Tableau will be using it.

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:36.910
And this is all
about our journey.

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:38.790
And thank you very much.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:41.540
